        <p class="hero__sub">
          Abu Dhabi runs two custody systems, one for non-Muslim parents and one for Muslim families.
          Here is which applies to you, how visits, travel and support work, and the process step by
          step. For all seven emirates, see our
          <a href="/en/child-custody-uae/">UAE child custody guide</a>.
        </p>
        <ul class="hero__trust">
          <li>✓ Both Abu Dhabi courts compared</li>
          <li>✓ Checked against ADJD rules</li>
          <li>✓ Updated for the 2024 law</li>
          <li>✓ Free, confidential consultation</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__form">
        <LeadForm lang="en" variant="hero" heading="Free Abu Dhabi Custody Consultation" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="container main-content">

    <!-- Short answer and court comparison -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="snapshot-heading">
      <h2 id="snapshot-heading">Child Custody Law in Abu Dhabi: The Short Answer</h2>
      <p>
        Child custody in Abu Dhabi depends on which court hears your case. Non-Muslim parents go to the
        <strong>Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</strong>, which applies Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 on
        Civil Marriage and its Effects and ADJD Chairman Resolution No. 8 of 2022, and starts from joint
        custody. Muslim parents go to the <strong>ADJD Personal Status Court</strong>, which applies
        Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024: the mother usually has day-to-day custody (hadana) until the
        child is 18, and the father stays the guardian.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both courts belong to the Abu Dhabi Judicial Department (ADJD), both decide on the
        child&rsquo;s best interests, and both can make urgent orders on travel, documents and visits.
      </p>
      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">2</span>
          <span class="stat-label">court tracks: civil and Sharia-based</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">16</span>
          <span class="stat-label">age the child chooses at the Civil Family Court</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">15</span>
          <span class="stat-label">age a child may choose under the 2024 law</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">18</span>
          <span class="stat-label">age Muslim custody ends, for boys and girls</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Question</th><th>Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</th><th>ADJD Personal Status Court</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Who it hears</td><td>Non-Muslims, some foreigners from countries that do not mainly apply Sharia in family matters, and civil marriages</td><td>Muslim families, UAE nationals and expats</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 and Resolution No. 8 of 2022</td><td>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, in force since 15 April 2025</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Starting point</td><td>Joint legal and physical custody, set in the divorce judgment</td><td>Mother usually has day-to-day custody; father is guardian</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Key ages</td><td>Equal joint custody until 16, then the child chooses; the child&rsquo;s wish counts from 12</td><td>Custody until 18; the child may choose from 15</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Family Guidance first?</td><td>No, cases go straight to a judge</td><td>Usually yes, except urgent requests</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Language</td><td>Arabic and English; can run fully in English</td><td>Arabic; documents translated into Arabic</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Travel abroad</td><td>Written consent approved by the court, or a judge&rsquo;s permission</td><td>Written consent, or court permission for up to 60 days a year</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Article references for each row appear in the sections below. For a wider comparison, see
          <a href="/en/dubai-vs-abu-dhabi-divorce/">Dubai vs Abu Dhabi divorce</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="info-box info-box--gold" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>Not sure which court is yours?</h3>
        <p>
          It is not always &ldquo;Muslim or not&rdquo;. Article 5 of Resolution No. 8 of 2022 also covers
          foreigners from countries that do not mainly apply Sharia in family matters, marriages
          concluded in such countries, and civil marriages. The court granted joint custody in a 2024 case
          involving an Australian Muslim wife, reported by The National, but the Court of Cassation has
          ruled both ways on Muslim dual nationals. Mixed or dual-national families should get advice on
          the forum first.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Joint custody at the Civil Family Court -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="joint-heading">
      <h2 id="joint-heading">How Joint Custody Works at the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</h2>
      <p>
        At the Civil Family Court, joint custody is automatic: the court writes it into the divorce
        judgment, and both parents keep equal rights until the child turns 16, when the child can choose
        where to live (Law No. 14 of 2021, Article 16; Resolution No. 8 of 2022, Articles 25 and 26). It
        covers legal custody, meaning big decisions such as school and medical treatment, and physical
        custody, the right to live with the child. The federal civil law used for non-Muslims in Dubai
        and the other emirates runs joint custody to 18, so do not mix up the two ages.
      </p>

      <h3>How parenting time is divided</h3>
      <p>
        Parents can agree their own schedule. If they cannot, the court divides time by alternating
        weeks, two-week blocks or months, or any pattern that suits the child, with weekends and public
        holidays set out on its form (Article 28). Week-on, week-off often suits school-age children
        living near both homes.
      </p>

      <h3>Asking for sole custody</h3>
      <p>
        A parent can waive joint custody, or ask the court to remove the other parent from legal
        custody, physical custody or both, using the Child Custody Dispute Application (Articles 29 and
        32). The grounds in Article 33 include:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>A risk of domestic violence or abuse, or living conditions that are not adequate for the child</li>
        <li>Behavioural, psychological or mental health problems that could harm or neglect the child</li>
        <li>Lack of time for the child, or neglect</li>
        <li>Abuse of drugs, alcohol or psychotropic substances</li>
        <li>The child&rsquo;s own wish, once the child is 12</li>
        <li>The other parent&rsquo;s remarriage, or any other reason the court accepts</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        While the application is pending, the court can suspend the joint arrangement or appoint a
        social expert. Waiving custody does not end a parent&rsquo;s financial duties (Article 30).
      </p>

      <h3>When parents disagree</h3>
      <p>
        If you disagree on a school, a treatment or a move, either parent can ask the court to
        intervene. It must decide quickly and keep the child&rsquo;s school and sports life as stable as
        possible (Articles 35 and 36).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Muslim custody -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="muslim-heading">
      <h2 id="muslim-heading">Custody for Muslim Families at the ADJD Personal Status Court</h2>
      <p>
        For Muslim families, Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, in force since 15 April 2025, runs
        custody (hadana) until age 18 for boys and girls (Article 123). From 15, the child may choose
        which parent to live with, and the court follows that choice unless it goes against the
        child&rsquo;s interests (Article 122).
      </p>

      <h3>Mother first, father as guardian</h3>
      <p>
        The mother comes first for day-to-day care, then the father, the maternal grandmother and the
        paternal grandmother, although the court can depart from that order for the child&rsquo;s sake
        (Article 114). Guardianship (wilaya) over the child&rsquo;s person and property normally stays
        with the father, while the custodial mother has educational guardianship for school matters
        (Article 112). Our
        <a href="/en/guardianship-vs-custody-uae/">guardianship vs custody guide</a> explains the split.
      </p>

      <h3>What can disqualify a custodian</h3>
      <p>Articles 113 and 115 set the conditions and the ways custody can be lost, including:</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Not being of sound mind, trustworthy and able to raise the child, or having a contagious or serious illness that puts the child at risk</li>
        <li>A conviction for a crime against honour or modesty, or addiction to drugs or alcohol</li>
        <li>A different religion from the child, although the court can let a non-Muslim mother keep custody</li>
        <li>For a mother, marriage to a man who is not closely related to the child, unless the court finds the child&rsquo;s interest points the other way</li>
        <li>Neglecting the child, or moving somewhere the child&rsquo;s interests are lost</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A person entitled to custody who waits more than a year after learning of the reason can lose
        the claim (Article 115). For remarriage in detail, see
        <a href="/en/custody-if-mother-remarries-uae/">whether a mother loses custody if she remarries</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>What changed from the old law</h3>
      <p>
        The 2024 law replaced Federal Law No. 28 of 2005, under which a mother&rsquo;s custody ended at
        about 11 for boys and 13 for girls. That repealed rule is gone, along with the old age-five limit
        for a non-Muslim mother, and the time to claim custody rose from six months to one year. More in
        <a href="/en/new-uae-personal-status-law-2025/">the new UAE personal status law</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Joint custody for Muslim parents?</h3>
      <p>
        Not by default, though parents can agree a shared schedule through Family Guidance. A 2026 Dubai
        Court of Cassation judgment, reported by a UAE law firm, upheld joint custody for a Muslim family;
        it does not bind Abu Dhabi courts. See our guide to
        <a href="/en/father-custody-rights-uae/">fathers&rsquo; and mothers&rsquo; custody rights</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Visitation -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="visitation-heading">
      <h2 id="visitation-heading">Visitation and Handovers in Abu Dhabi</h2>
      <p>
        Visitation rights in Abu Dhabi follow your written agreement or the court order: the parent
        without day-to-day custody can visit, take the child out and, where agreed or ordered, have
        overnight stays. Under the 2024 law the court decides if parents disagree (Article 121); at the
        Civil Family Court, the judge can also set temporary visits while a dispute runs (Article 42).
        Precise orders covering weekends, school holidays, Eid, video calls and a named handover place
        prevent most arguments. Our
        <a href="/en/visitation-rights-uae/">visitation rights guide</a> has schedules by age.
      </p>

      <h3>ADJD Child Visitation Centres</h3>
      <p>
        Where a court orders visits at a centre, ADJD runs Child Visitation Centres in Abu Dhabi, Al Ain
        and Al Dhafra, which supervise the carrying out of visitation rulings in a safe setting for the
        child. According to ADJD, visits run from Thursday to Saturday, 8am to 8.30pm, and the centres
        also register visitation files, take notice when a parent cannot attend and arrange virtual
        visits.
      </p>

      <h3>When the other parent does not comply</h3>
      <p>
        Keep a dated log and go back to the court rather than retaliating. The Civil Family Court
        enforces its own orders and can fine a parent, raise the fines for repeat breaches and deduct
        custody days (Articles 40, 41 and 46). At the Personal Status Court, the enforcement judge
        enforces visitation rulings compulsorily (Article 121). Repeated obstruction can support a
        custody change, and refusing to hand over a child as a court decision requires can be a crime
        under the federal Crimes and Penalties Law. For safety concerns, ask the court for supervised
        visits and read our <a href="/en/domestic-violence-divorce-uae/">domestic violence and divorce
        guide</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Travel and relocation -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="travel-heading">
      <h2 id="travel-heading">Travel, Passports and Relocation</h2>
      <p>
        Neither parent can decide alone to take a child abroad from Abu Dhabi: you need the other
        parent&rsquo;s written consent or a court&rsquo;s permission, and either parent can ask the court
        for a travel ban on the child.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Civil Family Court</h3>
          <p>
            Consent must be written and approved by the court on its form, with the dates, purpose and
            any guarantees. If the other parent objects, a judge decides and can require guarantees of
            return (Article 37). Either parent can seek a temporary child travel ban (Article 38), and
            ADJD&rsquo;s site describes a prohibited steps order for the same risk.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Personal Status Court</h3>
          <p>
            The custodian needs the other parent&rsquo;s written consent. If it is refused, the court can
            allow travel of up to 60 days a year, extendable for reasons such as medical treatment
            (Article 116). The guardian keeps the passport and hands it over for approved trips
            (Article 117).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Taking a child abroad without consent or permission is a criminal offence; Khaleej Times
        reported penalties of imprisonment and fines of AED 5,000 to 100,000 under the 2024 law. Our
        <a href="/en/travel-ban-divorce-uae/">travel ban guide</a> explains how bans are placed and
        lifted.
      </p>

      <h3>Moving within the UAE or abroad</h3>
      <p>
        A move to another emirate is judged by its effect on the child. At the Civil Family Court, a move
        that upsets the joint schedule should be agreed or put to the court (Article 35), and it can
        change where future applications are heard (Article 4). Under the 2024 law, moving somewhere the
        child&rsquo;s interests are lost can cost custody (Article 115).
      </p>
      <p>
        Moving abroad needs consent or a court order. The UAE is not a party to the 1980 Hague Child
        Abduction Convention, so there is no Hague return route if a child is taken from Abu Dhabi, or
        brought here. Clear consent terms, travel bans and mirror orders are your protection: see
        <a href="/en/child-relocation-after-divorce-uae/">child relocation after divorce</a> and
        <a href="/en/international-child-abduction-uae/">international child abduction</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Child support -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="support-heading">
      <h2 id="support-heading">Child Support in Abu Dhabi and How It Links to Custody</h2>
      <p>
        On both Abu Dhabi tracks, the father pays the children&rsquo;s maintenance after divorce,
        however custody and parenting time are divided.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the Civil Family Court, the father keeps covering education, residence fees, medical
        treatment and the other daily or periodic costs he paid before the divorce, in an amount the
        court sets with help from an accounting expert&rsquo;s report (Resolution No. 8 of 2022,
        Articles 23 and 24). Unless the court decides
        otherwise, he also pays the mother a housing allowance during joint custody, which can be cut if
        she can afford suitable housing herself (Article 19). At the Personal Status Court, claims
        typically cover child support, housing, school fees and a custody fee, and maintenance claims by
        the person entitled are exempt from ADJD court fees.
      </p>
      <p>
        Support and access are separate: late support does not justify blocking visits, and blocked
        visits do not justify stopping payment. See our <a href="/en/child-support-uae/">child support
        guide</a> and, for the wife&rsquo;s own claims, <a href="/en/alimony-abu-dhabi/">alimony in Abu
        Dhabi</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Process -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="process-heading">
      <h2 id="process-heading">The Child Custody Process in Abu Dhabi, Step by Step</h2>
      <p>
        Non-Muslim parents file directly with the Civil Family Court, while Muslim parents usually start
        at ADJD&rsquo;s Family Guidance section and reach a judge only if no agreement is made. If you
        have not filed for divorce yet, see
        <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Civil Family Court route (non-Muslims)</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Divorce with joint custody</h3>
          <p>
            Joint custody is written into the no-fault divorce judgment (Article 26). The application
            costs AED 5,000, and ADJD says it aims to finish divorce cases within about a month.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>File a Child Custody Dispute Application</h3>
          <p>
            To object, remove a parent or settle a disagreement, file the ADJD form, usually through a
            typing centre or a lawyer. English documents are accepted (Article 44).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Interim orders, hearing and decision</h3>
          <p>
            Ask for urgent orders on visits, documents or travel (Article 42; see our
            <a href="/en/emergency-orders-divorce-uae/">emergency orders guide</a>). The court may appoint
            a social expert, then decides on the child&rsquo;s best interests.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Personal Status Court route (Muslims)</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Open a Family Guidance file</h3>
          <p>
            Register through the ADJD website, a law firm or a typing centre; sessions run by video
            link. See <a href="/en/family-guidance-section-uae/">how Family Guidance works</a>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Settle, or get referred to court</h3>
          <p>
            An agreement is documented and endorsed as a binding family agreement. Without one, the case
            is referred to court and you file a claim with the custody application attached.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Use urgent orders where needed</h3>
          <p>
            A child travel ban or interim custody or support can go straight to a judge; the 2024 law
            exempts urgent custody and maintenance orders from referral.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Hearings and judgment</h3>
          <p>
            The court can order a social or psychological assessment, then rules on custody, visits and
            support. Either side can appeal.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Documents, timeline and costs</h3>
      <p>
        Bring Emirates IDs and passports, the marriage certificate and any divorce judgment, the
        children&rsquo;s birth certificates, existing orders, school and medical records, and salary
        certificates and bank statements if support is disputed. The Personal Status Court needs
        certified Arabic translations and attested foreign documents; see our
        <a href="/en/documents-for-divorce-uae/">divorce documents checklist</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Indicatively, an agreed arrangement can be approved within weeks, while a contested custody case
        at the Personal Status Court commonly takes three to twelve months at first instance, longer with
        appeals. These ranges come from law-firm guidance, not ADJD figures.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Item</th><th>Fee</th><th>Source</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Family Guidance file on custody, visits or documents</td><td>AED 500</td><td>ADJD service manual</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Personal status claim at court</td><td>AED 50 per request, capped at AED 500, plus AED 300 per defendant for notice</td><td>ADJD service manual</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Urgent personal status order</td><td>AED 100</td><td>ADJD service manual</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Civil Family Court divorce application</td><td>AED 5,000</td><td>Resolution No. 8 of 2022</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sworn translation</td><td>AED 150 to 500 per document</td><td>Indicative, law-firm guides</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Custody or psychological assessment</td><td>AED 3,000 to 10,000</td><td>Indicative, law-firm guides</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Lawyer fees</td><td>AED 5,000 to 10,000 if agreed; AED 8,000 to 25,000 if contested, more with appeals</td><td>Indicative, law-firm fee guides</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Official fees are from the 2023 ADJD service manual and Resolution No. 8 of 2022; confirm the
          current amount when you file. See the <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">total cost of divorce</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What the court looks at -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="factors-heading">
      <h2 id="factors-heading">What the Court Looks At, and What Hurts a Custody Case</h2>
      <p>
        Both Abu Dhabi courts decide custody on the best interests of the child. ADJD&rsquo;s Civil
        Family Court page lists what a judge weighs, and the same themes of stability, schooling, health
        and conduct run through the Personal Status Court&rsquo;s approach:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>The child&rsquo;s physical, emotional and educational needs</li>
        <li>Age, background and other relevant characteristics</li>
        <li>The child&rsquo;s wishes and feelings, if old enough to understand</li>
        <li>The likely effect of any change, and any harm the child has suffered or may suffer</li>
        <li>Each parent&rsquo;s willingness to support the child&rsquo;s bond with the other parent</li>
        <li>Each parent&rsquo;s ability to meet the child&rsquo;s needs</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Evidence that helps</h3>
          <p>
            School and medical records, a calendar of who handles school runs and appointments, suitable
            housing near the school, a workable childcare plan and calm, child-focused messages.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Mistakes that hurt</h3>
          <p>
            Moving or travelling with the child without consent, keeping the child after a visit,
            criticising the other parent online, ignoring orders, stopping support for leverage, and
            secretly recording calls, which can breach UAE privacy and cybercrime laws.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Posts about the other parent or the child can end up in the court file; read
        <a href="/en/social-media-child-custody-uae/">social media and child custody</a> before you post.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Lawyer -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lawyer-heading">
      <h2 id="lawyer-heading">When to Get an Abu Dhabi Custody Lawyer, and What to Ask</h2>
      <p>
        Get a child custody lawyer in Abu Dhabi before you file or sign a parenting plan, and at once if
        the other parent threatens to take the child abroad or there are safety concerns.
      </p>
      <p>
        A lawyer earns the fee when custody is contested, one parent is Muslim and the other is not, or
        relocation is on the table. Foreign lawyers can act at the Civil Family Court once registered on
        ADJD&rsquo;s foreign lawyers&rsquo; roll (Article 45); at the Personal Status Court, filings are
        in Arabic, so a UAE-licensed advocate is the usual choice. Questions to ask:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Which court and law apply to our family, and why?</li>
        <li>How often do you appear at the Abu Dhabi family courts, and who will attend my hearings?</li>
        <li>Which interim orders should I seek now, on travel, documents or visits?</li>
        <li>How do you charge, and are translation and expert fees included?</li>
        <li>Will you confirm the quote and scope in writing?</li>
      </ul>
